1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Section 3

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,369
LIZZIE:

3
00:00:03,369 --> 00:00:06,973
So how are you getting on with your teaching practice at the High School,

4
00:00:06,973 --> 00:00:08,000
Joe?

5
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:08,863
JOE:

6
00:00:08,863 --> 00:00:11,853
Well I've been put in charge of the school marching band,

7
00:00:11,853 --> 00:00:14,020
and it's quite a responsibility.

8
00:00:14,020 --> 00:00:16,850
I'd like to talk it over with you.

9
00:00:16,850 --> 00:00:16,900
LIZZIE:

10
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:18,038
Go ahead.

11
00:00:18,038 --> 00:00:22,000
You'd better start by giving me a bit of background.

12
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:22,397
JOE:

13
00:00:22,397 --> 00:00:22,793
OK.

14
00:00:22,793 --> 00:00:26,759
Well the band has students in it from all years,

15
00:00:26,759 --> 00:00:29,139
so they're aged 11 to 18,

16
00:00:29,139 --> 00:00:32,312
and there are about 50 of them altogether.

17
00:00:32,312 --> 00:00:35,484
It's quite a popular activity within the school.

18
00:00:35,484 --> 00:00:39,847
I've never worked with a band of more than 20 before,

19
00:00:39,847 --> 00:00:41,830
and this is very different.

20
00:00:41,830 --> 00:00:42,398
LIZZIE:

21
00:00:42,398 --> 00:00:44,100
I can imagine.

22
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:44,472
JOE:

23
00:00:44,472 --> 00:00:48,196
They aren't really good enough to enter national band competitions,

24
00:00:48,196 --> 00:00:51,919
but they're in a regional one later in the term.

25
00:00:51,919 --> 00:00:53,780
Even if they don't win,

26
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:59,365
and I don't expect them to, hopefully it'll be an incentive for them to try

27
00:00:59,365 --> 00:01:00,110
and improve.

28
00:01:00,110 --> 00:01:01,027
LIZZIE:

29
00:01:01,027 --> 00:01:02,860
Yes, hopefully.

30
00:01:02,860 --> 00:01:03,216
JOE:

31
00:01:03,216 --> 00:01:07,128
Well, now the town council's organising a carnival in the summer,

32
00:01:07,128 --> 00:01:09,972
and the band has been asked to perform.

33
00:01:09,972 --> 00:01:13,884
If you ask me, they aren't really up to it yet,

34
00:01:13,884 --> 00:01:17,796
and I need to get them functioning better as a band,

35
00:01:17,796 --> 00:01:19,930
and in a very short time.

36
00:01:19,930 --> 00:01:20,322
LIZZIE:

37
00:01:20,322 --> 00:01:23,066
Have you been doing anything with them?

38
00:01:23,066 --> 00:01:25,810
Apart from practising the music, I mean.

39
00:01:25,810 --> 00:01:26,159
JOE:

40
00:01:26,159 --> 00:01:31,039
I played a recording I came across, of a drummer talking about how playing

41
00:01:31,039 --> 00:01:33,479
in a band had changed his life.

42
00:01:33,479 --> 00:01:35,919
I think it was an after-dinner speech.

43
00:01:35,919 --> 00:01:38,010
I thought it was pretty inspiring,

44
00:01:38,010 --> 00:01:42,541
because being in the band had stopped him from getting involved in crime.

45
00:01:42,541 --> 00:01:45,330
The students seemed to find it interesting, too.

46
00:01:45,330 --> 00:01:45,913
LIZZIE:

47
00:01:45,913 --> 00:01:47,080
That's good.

48
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:47,447
JOE:

49
00:01:47,447 --> 00:01:52,591
I'm planning to show them that old film from the 1940s 'Strike Up the

50
00:01:52,591 --> 00:01:52,959
Band',

51
00:01:52,959 --> 00:01:55,530
and talk about it with the students.

52
00:01:55,530 --> 00:01:57,000
What do you think?

53
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:57,394
LIZZIE:

54
00:01:57,394 --> 00:01:58,181
Good idea.

55
00:01:58,181 --> 00:02:03,694
As it's about a school band, it might make the students realise how much

56
00:02:03,694 --> 00:02:06,450
they can achieve if they work together.

57
00:02:06,450 --> 00:02:06,761
JOE:

58
00:02:06,761 --> 00:02:08,624
That's what I've got in mind.

59
00:02:08,624 --> 00:02:12,970
I'm hoping I can take some of the band to a parade that's going

60
00:02:12,970 --> 00:02:14,523
to take place next month.

61
00:02:14,523 --> 00:02:17,007
A couple of marching bands will be performing,

62
00:02:17,007 --> 00:02:19,180
and the atmosphere should be quite exciting.

63
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:23,217
It depends on whether I can persuade the school to hire a coach

64
00:02:23,217 --> 00:02:25,080
or two to take us there.

65
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:25,481
LIZZIE:

66
00:02:25,481 --> 00:02:25,882
Mmm.

67
00:02:25,882 --> 00:02:28,690
They sound like good ideas to me.

68
00:02:28,690 --> 00:02:29,740
JOE:

69
00:02:29,740 --> 00:02:30,790
Thanks.

70
00:02:30,790 --> 00:02:31,083
JOE:

71
00:02:31,083 --> 00:02:35,182
Can I tell you about a few people in the band who I'm finding

72
00:02:35,182 --> 00:02:36,939
it quite difficult to cope with?

73
00:02:36,939 --> 00:02:40,160
I'm sure you'll have some ideas about what I can do.

74
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:40,707
LIZZIE:

75
00:02:40,707 --> 00:02:41,800
Go ahead.

76
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:42,155
JOE:

77
00:02:42,155 --> 00:02:46,059
There's a flautist who says she loves playing in the band.

78
00:02:46,059 --> 00:02:48,543
We rehearse twice a week after school,

79
00:02:48,543 --> 00:02:50,318
but she's hardly ever there.

80
00:02:50,318 --> 00:02:55,287
Then she looks for me the next day and gives me a very plausible

81
00:02:55,287 --> 00:02:58,481
reason - she says she had to help her mother,

82
00:02:58,481 --> 00:02:59,901
or she's been ill,

83
00:02:59,901 --> 00:03:02,740
but to be honest, I don't believe her.

84
00:03:02,740 --> 00:03:03,415
LIZZIE:

85
00:03:03,415 --> 00:03:04,765
Oh dear!

86
00:03:04,765 --> 00:03:08,140
Any more students with difficulties?

87
00:03:08,140 --> 00:03:08,554
JOE:

88
00:03:08,554 --> 00:03:08,969
Plenty!

89
00:03:08,969 --> 00:03:14,771
There's a trumpeter who thinks she's the best musician in the band, though she

90
00:03:14,771 --> 00:03:15,599
certainly isn't.

91
00:03:15,599 --> 00:03:19,744
She's always saying what she thinks other people should do,

92
00:03:19,744 --> 00:03:22,230
which makes my job pretty difficult.

93
00:03:22,230 --> 00:03:22,602
LIZZIE:

94
00:03:22,602 --> 00:03:25,210
She sounds a bit of a nightmare!

95
00:03:25,210 --> 00:03:25,568
JOE:

96
00:03:25,568 --> 00:03:27,360
You can say that again.

97
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:31,303
One of the trombonists has got an impressive sense of rhythm,

98
00:03:31,303 --> 00:03:35,604
and could be an excellent musician - except that he has breathing difficulties,

99
00:03:35,604 --> 00:03:39,188
and he doesn't really have enough breath for the trombone.

100
00:03:39,188 --> 00:03:42,414
He'd be much better off playing percussion, for instance,

101
00:03:42,414 --> 00:03:44,564
but he refuses to give up.

102
00:03:44,564 --> 00:03:47,790
So he ends up only playing half the notes.

103
00:03:47,790 --> 00:03:48,263
LIZZIE:

104
00:03:48,263 --> 00:03:52,050
I suppose you have to admire his determination.

105
00:03:52,050 --> 00:03:52,398
JOE:

106
00:03:52,398 --> 00:03:53,094
Hmm, maybe.

107
00:03:53,094 --> 00:03:55,531
One of the percussionists isn't too bad,

108
00:03:55,531 --> 00:03:58,664
but he never seems to interact with other people,

109
00:03:58,664 --> 00:04:02,493
and he always rushes off as soon as the rehearsal ends.

110
00:04:02,493 --> 00:04:05,278
I don't know if there are family reasons,

111
00:04:05,278 --> 00:04:05,974
or what.

112
00:04:05,974 --> 00:04:10,848
But it isn't good in a band, where people really need to feel they're

113
00:04:10,848 --> 00:04:12,240
part of a group.

114
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:13,315
LIZZIE:

115
00:04:13,315 --> 00:04:14,390
Hmm.

116
00:04:14,390 --> 00:04:14,690
JOE:

117
00:04:14,690 --> 00:04:15,890
There are others too,

118
00:04:15,890 --> 00:04:19,790
but at least that gives you an idea of what l'm up against.

119
00:04:19,790 --> 00:04:23,090
Do you have any thoughts about what I can do, Lizzie?
